<h2 id="carfax">Carfax: Full Review</h2>

<p><a href="/go/carfax/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carfax</a> is the most recognized name in vehicle history reports, and for good reason. Founded in 1984, the company has spent decades building relationships with data providers and establishing itself as the industry standard.</p>

<h3>Data Sources and Coverage</h3>

<p>Carfax claims to aggregate data from more than 130,000 sources, including:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>State DMV offices:</strong> Title registrations, transfers, and brand history</li>
  <li><strong>Insurance companies:</strong> Collision claims and total loss declarations</li>
  <li><strong>Auto dealers:</strong> Sales, trades, and lease returns</li>
  <li><strong>Service and repair facilities:</strong> Maintenance records and repair orders</li>
  <li><strong>Inspection stations:</strong> State inspection and emissions testing records</li>
  <li><strong>Auto auctions:</strong> Vehicle condition reports and auction listings</li>
  <li><strong>Fleet management companies:</strong> Commercial and rental vehicle history</li>
  <li><strong>Fire and police departments:</strong> Accident and incident reports</li>
  <li><strong>Collision repair shops:</strong> Body work and structural repairs</li>
</ul>

<h3>What a Carfax Report Provides</h3>

<p>A typical Carfax report includes:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Accident history:</strong> Reported collisions with severity indicators (minor, moderate, severe)</li>
  <li><strong>Title information:</strong> Clean, salvage, rebuilt, flood, hail, lemon law, etc.</li>
  <li><strong>Ownership history:</strong> Number of previous owners and states registered</li>
  <li><strong>Service records:</strong> Maintenance performed at participating shops and dealers</li>
  <li><strong>Odometer readings:</strong> Historical readings to detect rollback fraud</li>
  <li><strong>Use type:</strong> Personal, rental, fleet, lease, taxi, police</li>
  <li><strong>Structural damage indicators:</strong> Frame or structural component replacement</li>
  <li><strong>Airbag deployment records:</strong> When available from insurance claims</li>
  <li><strong>Recall status:</strong> Open and completed recalls</li>
  <li><strong>Detailed vehicle specifications:</strong> Trim, engine, options</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pricing</h3>

<p>Carfax offers several pricing tiers:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Single Report:</strong> $44.99 for one VIN</li>
  <li><strong>3-Report Package:</strong> $59.99 (effectively $20 per report)</li>
  <li><strong>Unlimited Reports:</strong> $99.99 for 60 days of unlimited reports</li>
</ul>

<p>The unlimited package is ideal for dealers or buyers shopping extensively. The 3-report package is best for consumers comparing a few finalists.</p>

<h3>Carfax Buyback Guarantee</h3>

<p>Carfax offers a buyback guarantee for vehicles with clean titles that later turn out to have a branded title (salvage, flood, etc.) that should have appeared in the report. However, this guarantee has significant limitations:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Only applies to title brands, not undisclosed accidents</li>
  <li>Must discover the problem within 90 days of purchase</li>
  <li>Maximum payout is the original purchase price</li>
  <li>Only applies if the title brand existed before your purchase and should have been reported</li>
  <li>Doesn't cover mechanical problems, odometer fraud, or condition issues</li>
</ul>

<p>The guarantee is essentially insurance against Carfax's own data gaps—not a warranty on the vehicle itself.</p>

<h3>Strengths</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Largest database:</strong> More data sources than any competitor</li>
  <li><strong>Service records:</strong> Superior coverage of dealer and shop maintenance</li>
  <li><strong>Brand recognition:</strong> Universally accepted by buyers, sellers, and lenders</li>
  <li><strong>User interface:</strong> Easy-to-read reports with clear severity indicators</li>
  <li><strong>Vehicle valuation:</strong> Integration with market pricing data</li>
</ul>

<h3>Weaknesses</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Expensive:</strong> At $44.99 per report, it's the most costly option</li>
  <li><strong>False sense of security:</strong> "Clean Carfax" has become shorthand for a problem-free vehicle, which is often not true</li>
  <li><strong>Incomplete data:</strong> Only reports what has been reported to their sources—many accidents and repairs go unreported</li>
  <li><strong>Regional gaps:</strong> Coverage varies significantly by state and region</li>
  <li><strong>Cash repair blind spots:</strong> Private party repairs paid in cash never appear</li>
</ul>

<div class="warning-box">
  <h4>Important Limitation</h4>
  <p>A clean Carfax report does not mean the vehicle has never been in an accident. It only means no accidents have been reported to Carfax's data sources. Minor accidents, private party repairs, and cash transactions frequently go unreported.</p>
</div>

<h3>When to Use Carfax</h3>

<p>Carfax is worth the investment when:</p>

<ul>
  <li>You're seriously considering purchasing a specific vehicle</li>
  <li>The vehicle is from a dealer or region with good reporting coverage</li>
  <li>You want comprehensive service and maintenance history</li>
  <li>You're comparing multiple finalists (use the 3-report package)</li>
  <li>The seller advertises "clean Carfax" and you want to verify</li>
</ul>

<p><a href="/go/carfax/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get a Carfax Report</a></p>

<h2 id="autocheck">AutoCheck: Full Review</h2>

<p>AutoCheck, owned by Experian (one of the three major credit bureaus), is Carfax's primary competitor. While it doesn't have Carfax's brand recognition, it offers similar data coverage at a significantly lower price point.</p>

<h3>Data Sources and Coverage</h3>

<p>AutoCheck aggregates data from sources including:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>State DMV records:</strong> Title history and registrations</li>
  <li><strong>Insurance companies:</strong> Collision and total loss claims</li>
  <li><strong>Auto auctions:</strong> Particularly strong coverage of Manheim auction data</li>
  <li><strong>Salvage yards and junkyards:</strong> Total loss and disposal records</li>
  <li><strong>Rental and fleet companies:</strong> Commercial vehicle history</li>
  <li><strong>Fire and police departments:</strong> Incident reports</li>
  <li><strong>Service facilities:</strong> Limited maintenance records</li>
</ul>

<h3>What an AutoCheck Report Provides</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Accident records:</strong> Reported collisions and damage</li>
  <li><strong>Title information:</strong> Brands, transfers, and state registrations</li>
  <li><strong>Odometer readings:</strong> Historical tracking</li>
  <li><strong>Auction history:</strong> Superior auction data from Manheim</li>
  <li><strong>Ownership history:</strong> Number of owners and duration</li>
  <li><strong>Use type:</strong> Rental, fleet, personal, etc.</li>
  <li><strong>AutoCheck Score:</strong> Proprietary 1-100 rating system</li>
  <li><strong>Recall information:</strong> Open recalls</li>
  <li><strong>Structural damage indicators:</strong> Frame damage when reported</li>
</ul>

<h3>The AutoCheck Score</h3>

<p>AutoCheck's unique feature is its proprietary scoring system, which assigns each vehicle a score from 1 to 100 based on its history. The score considers:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Number and severity of accidents</li>
  <li>Title brands and transfers</li>
  <li>Odometer consistency</li>
  <li>Number of owners</li>
  <li>Vehicle age and mileage</li>
  <li>Comparison to similar vehicles</li>
</ul>

<p>The score provides a quick at-a-glance assessment, but you should still review the detailed report. A low score doesn't necessarily mean you should avoid the vehicle—it depends on what issues affected the score and their severity.</p>

<h3>Pricing</h3>

<p>AutoCheck's pricing is significantly more affordable than Carfax:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Single Report:</strong> $24.99 per VIN</li>
  <li><strong>25 Reports:</strong> $49.99 (effectively $2 per report)</li>
</ul>

<p>The 25-report package is ideal for dealers or buyers who are actively shopping and want to run reports on every vehicle they consider. At $2 per report, there's little reason not to check every candidate.</p>

<h3>Strengths</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Affordability:</strong> Significantly cheaper than Carfax</li>
  <li><strong>Auction data:</strong> Superior coverage of Manheim auction sales and condition reports</li>
  <li><strong>AutoCheck Score:</strong> Quick way to assess overall vehicle history</li>
  <li><strong>Bulk pricing:</strong> 25-report package makes checking multiple vehicles economical</li>
  <li><strong>Experian backing:</strong> Owned by a major credit bureau with data expertise</li>
</ul>

<h3>Weaknesses</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Less brand recognition:</strong> Not as widely known or accepted as Carfax</li>
  <li><strong>Fewer service records:</strong> Doesn't track dealer maintenance as comprehensively</li>
  <li><strong>Interface:</strong> Report layout is less intuitive than Carfax</li>
  <li><strong>Regional variation:</strong> Coverage quality varies by location</li>
</ul>

<h3>When to Use AutoCheck</h3>

<p>AutoCheck is the best choice when:</p>

<ul>
  <li>You're on a budget and want to check multiple vehicles</li>
  <li>The vehicle has been through auctions (especially Manheim)</li>
  <li>You want to screen many candidates before narrowing your list</li>
  <li>You're a dealer or high-volume buyer</li>
  <li>You want a second opinion after reviewing a Carfax report</li>
</ul>

<div class="tip-box">
  <h4>Pro Tip</h4>
  <p>For serious candidates, consider running both Carfax and AutoCheck. Each service has slightly different data sources, and one report may reveal information the other misses. The combined cost ($70) is still less than the average cost of an undiscovered problem.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="nmvtis">NMVTIS Reports</h2>

<p>The National Motor Vehicle Title Information System (NMVTIS) is a federal database created by the Department of Justice to prevent title fraud and protect consumers from unsafe vehicles.</p>

<h3>What NMVTIS Covers</h3>

<p>NMVTIS aggregates data from:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>All state DMVs:</strong> Title brands, transfers, and registrations</li>
  <li><strong>Insurance carriers:</strong> Total loss declarations</li>
  <li><strong>Junk and salvage yards:</strong> Vehicle disposal records</li>
  <li><strong>Auto recyclers:</strong> Parts vehicle records</li>
</ul>

<p>NMVTIS focuses exclusively on title history and does not include:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Accident details or severity</li>
  <li>Service and maintenance records</li>
  <li>Odometer trending (only current reading)</li>
  <li>Recall information</li>
  <li>Ownership count or duration</li>
</ul>

<h3>What an NMVTIS Report Provides</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Current title brand:</strong> Clean, salvage, flood, rebuilt, etc.</li>
  <li><strong>Title history:</strong> Previous brands across all states</li>
  <li><strong>Total loss records:</strong> Insurance company declarations</li>
  <li><strong>Salvage yard reporting:</strong> If vehicle was junked or scrapped</li>
  <li><strong>Current odometer reading:</strong> Most recent reading on file</li>
  <li><strong>VIN validation:</strong> Confirms VIN is legitimate</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pricing</h3>

<p>NMVTIS reports cost approximately $10 per VIN, depending on the approved data provider. The official NMVTIS website maintains a list of approved providers.</p>

<h3>Strengths</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Comprehensive title data:</strong> Covers all 50 states</li>
  <li><strong>Federal oversight:</strong> Regulated by the Department of Justice</li>
  <li><strong>Inexpensive:</strong> Around $10 per report</li>
  <li><strong>Title fraud prevention:</strong> Best tool for detecting title washing across state lines</li>
  <li><strong>Legal status:</strong> Often required for titling in some states</li>
</ul>

<h3>Weaknesses</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Limited scope:</strong> Title information only—no accident details</li>
  <li><strong>No context:</strong> Doesn't explain what damage caused the brand</li>
  <li><strong>No service history:</strong> No maintenance or repair records</li>
  <li><strong>Basic interface:</strong> Reports are text-heavy and harder to interpret</li>
  <li><strong>Reporting delays:</strong> State DMVs may not update immediately</li>
</ul>

<h3>When to Use NMVTIS</h3>

<p>NMVTIS reports are ideal when:</p>

<ul>
  <li>You specifically want to verify title status</li>
  <li>You're concerned about title washing or fraud</li>
  <li>The vehicle has been registered in multiple states</li>
  <li>You want an inexpensive verification before investing in a full report</li>
  <li>You're purchasing from a private party with limited documentation</li>
</ul>

<p>For more information on title brands, see our <a href="/guides/title/salvage-title-guide/">Salvage Title Guide</a>.</p>

